{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact head cfcea2e10adc35eca4b73e499e7951c27bbd0946 on request/custody-lost-update-current. The mechanism is right and I could not defeat the lock itself; the problems are around it, and two of them are regressions against this head's own parent.\n\nWHAT PASSES. In a clean detached worktree at the exact head: git status empty, git diff --check exit 0. go test ./internal/apphost/... ./internal/app/... -count=1 passes (apphost 0.358s, app 55.723s). go vet ./... exit 0. gofmt -l clean on all six changed Go files. go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 passes (54.912s). go test -race on both packages passes in 1:07. Every gate this head must pass, passes.\n\nCLAIMS I VERIFIED RATHER THAN READ FOR. The exclusive advisory lock does real work: 32 concurrent in-process updaters each granting a distinct actor all survived, and so did 8 separate OS processes. The .config.lock file is empty, 0600, sits beside config.json, is never renamed, and is released by the kernel when its holder is SIGKILLed mid-hold, with the waiter then proceeding. The head is genuinely recut: its parent is a828c703c, which is current main.\n\nBLOCKING 1 - DATA RACE, a regression against the parent. app.go:529 `w.config = merged` is the only whole-struct write to w.config in the repository; `grep 'w\\.config = ' internal/app/*.go` finds it at the head and finds nothing on parent a828c703c. I checked both. The struct's own comment at app.go:66-71 declares that the scalar fields - Genesis, ObjectFormat, ReadOnly, SequencerKey, PayloadCeiling, IdempotencyNamespace, Version - \"are written once before the workspace is shared and never again, so reading them takes no lock\". Adopting the reloaded config writes all of them, and they are read unlocked in roughly twenty-five places, including EventID at app.go:1355, the act-building path at app.go:1106-1124, the `if w.config.ReadOnly` append gate at app.go:1203, checkpoint.go and resident.go.\n\nThis is not theoretical. It reproduces under -race with the workspace's own background snapshot goroutine as the writer: read at app.(*Workspace).EventID app.go:1355, previous write at app.(*Workspace).updateConfig app.go:529 via rememberVerifiedFrontier app.go:1682, snapshotWithSource app.go:1513, snapshotFlight app.go:1438. The identical probe on the parent is clean. SequencerKey is a string, so a torn read pairs a pointer with a length from a different value. make race is in CI, so this sits on a gate the project runs; it is green today only because no existing test exercises the interleaving.\n\nA two-line fix restores the documented invariant and was validated green, including the race probe: replace the whole-struct assignment with `w.config.Actors = merged.Actors` and `w.config.VerifiedFrontier = merged.VerifiedFrontier`. Holding configMu for the scalar reads would also work but is a much larger change.\n\nBLOCKING 2 - BUILD BREAK on solaris and aix, also a regression. I ran this myself at both commits. At the head, `GOOS=solaris GOARCH=amd64 go build ./internal/apphost/` fails with undefined: syscall.Flock and undefined: syscall.LOCK_EX at config_lock_unix.go:28 and :32, and `GOOS=aix GOARCH=ppc64` fails the same way. On parent a828c703c both build silently. The `//go:build unix` tag covers aix, solaris and illumos, but syscall.Flock does not exist on solaris or aix. darwin, linux, illumos, freebsd, openbsd, netbsd, dragonfly, android, windows, plan9, js and wasip1 all build fine at the head.\n\nThis defeats the change's own stated design rather than merely failing to compile: those two platforms are exactly the ones that should receive the loud runtime refusal from config_lock_other.go. CI is ubuntu-only, so nothing catches it. Either enumerate the platforms that actually have syscall.Flock in the build tag and let the rest fall through to the refusal, or use golang.org/x/sys/unix.Flock, which does cover solaris and aix.\n\nSHOULD FIX 3 - two statements are false in the paragraphs this head edited. docs/reference/architecture.md still says the in-memory configuration lock serializes custody mutation \"together with its save and rollback\". At this head the save happens inside apphost.UpdateConfig with configMu released, and there is no rollback anywhere - both AddActor and RetireActor dropped theirs. The configMu field comment at app.go:65-80 is stale in three ways: it claims configMu guards the save and the rollback; it says AddActor and RetireActor take configMu around their read-modify-save sections when neither takes it at all now; and it omits the new load-bearing fact that mutate runs under the apphost advisory lock with configMu not held. That last is exactly the ordering a future maintainer needs. updateConfig's own doc comment states it correctly, so the fix is small.\n\nSHOULD FIX 4 - the headline mechanism has no test. Eight mutations were applied at the real call sites, each reverted before the next. Killed: removing the reload from UpdateConfig, making RetireActor's mutate never delete, and never adopting merged into memory. Survived: making withConfigLock take no lock at all; accepting instead of refusing a mergeVerifiedFrontier conflict; removing AddActor's different-custody refusal; ignoring `changed` and always writing; letting a shallower audit overwrite a deeper on-disk frontier.\n\nThe second of those is the one that matters: withConfigLock taking no lock at all leaves the whole suite green. The cross-process lock, which is the entire point of this change, has zero coverage. The probe kills it decisively - without the lock, 2 of 32 in-process grants and 1 of 8 cross-process grants survive - and the in-process version runs in about ten milliseconds. Please add it.\n\nRelatedly, TestUpdateConfigNoChangeWritesNothing does not test what its name says. It fails with \"a no-change update rewrote the file\" but compares only file content, and a rewrite produces identical bytes, so the assertion cannot fail. It needs mtime or inode.\n\nNON-BLOCKING, recorded for the author's judgement. UpdateConfig's doc comment claims a second creator merges rather than overwrites; that holds only between UpdateConfig callers, since CreateConfig takes no lock, and a config hard-linked into place inside the lock window is then clobbered by SaveConfig's rename. No reachable production interleaving was found, because all three updateConfig call sites run on an opened workspace whose file already exists, so this is a false claim in an exported contract rather than a live defect. The lock path follows a planted symlink; O_NOFOLLOW would close it cheaply, though metaDir is 0700 and an attacker with write access there can edit config.json directly. Unlinking .config.lock breaks exclusion, which is inherent to lockfile designs. And architecture.md's \"Where no advisory locking exists, updates refuse loudly\" overstates what is enforced: the build-tag refusal covers a platform with no locking API, not a filesystem such as NFS where flock may be node-local or a silent no-op. That last was not reproduced - no such mount was available - and is stated as a limitation, not a finding.\n\nAlso out of scope and suspected rather than reproduced: generateActor still uses stat-then-WriteFile rather than O_EXCL. The new different-custody refusal changes the outcome of that narrow race, so what used to be a lost update now leaves the winner's config entry pointing at a key file the loser overwrote. That belongs in a child request, not this one.\n\nTHE THREE CONCLUSIONS.\n\nArchitecture. The affected layers are layer 4, Application host binding, in internal/apphost, and the internal/app boundary adapter. The contract changed: a new exported UpdateConfig, a new persistent .config.lock artifact in metaDir, and a new platform-conditional refusal. docs/reference/architecture.md was updated in this same head, correctly, in the layer-4 custody paragraph, and the four custody paths are now genuinely separated - that part is good work. But the page retains a sentence about the in-memory lock that is false at this head, in the paragraph the head edited, and this repository's own review rule makes that enough to request changes. Layer direction is preserved: internal/apphost gains no application ontology and internal/app remains the coupling point.\n\nSecurity. I examined the cross-process config lock, reload-and-merge persistence, the lock path, permission modes, and platform and filesystem failure modes. The change is a net improvement on the boundary it targets: the lost-update erasure of recorded custody is genuinely closed, verified across real OS processes. mutate is closure-supplied by three in-package call sites and takes no untrusted input; the lock file is never written to, so its content is not an input; permissions are 0600; and failure is fail-closed everywhere - no change means no write, a mutate error means no write, a lock error means no write. Two defects sit on this boundary rather than beside it. The data race touches the unlocked ReadOnly append gate and the SequencerKey string. The solaris and aix build break converts an intended loud refusal into a compile failure, which is the opposite of failing loudly at the moment of use.\n\nSimplification. Three, none of which weakens the conditions of satisfaction. Adopting only Actors and VerifiedFrontier is both simpler than the whole-struct assignment and truer to the documented invariant, and it is the race fix. withConfigLock[T any] is generic but only ever instantiated at Config; a plain non-generic signature drops the type parameter and the three `var zero T` lines from both files. And sha1ID(rune_ byte) takes a byte, not a rune, with an unidiomatic trailing underscore. Separately, save()'s new comment says \"Only the bootstrap creation path uses it\", but internal/app/atomic_file_test.go:35 calls it from 32 goroutines.\n\nREQUIRED FOR APPROVAL: fix the data race; fix the solaris and aix build and confirm with both GOOS settings; correct the two stale statements and record the new lock order in the struct comment; add a concurrency test that fails when withConfigLock takes no lock; and make TestUpdateConfigNoChangeWritesNothing able to fail. The remaining coverage gaps and the UpdateConfig comment correction are cheap and strongly recommended, but I would not block on them alone.\n\nI verified the two blocking findings at the commit myself before filing: the build failures at both GOOS targets on this head against a silent parent, and the presence of the sole whole-struct write against its absence on the parent alongside the struct comment that forbids it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b901d6944a25e9f41c2b21f6f25e161724c253b7","head":"cfcea2e10adc35eca4b73e499e7951c27bbd0946","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:44c5946a0b33b4b0024e90cede424a84e5357395, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6dd8f7530369b6eee942218e0d9efb634fa2c843, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:97e83a53dec3e66fe95aa78fe74f833e7a52835d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:cbb6cc9b32b2db6f9f2137dbf2acdd4d1e979927 and 1 more","verdict":"changes-requested"}}